NOTICE — 08-modern-attention-variants
=====================================

WHAT THIS IS

A coursework exercise: a sourced chronology of attention mechanisms and
positional encoding schemes, ordered by the date each first appeared publicly,
with the trade-offs each one makes. It is a survey and a study, not a product,
not a proposal to any organisation, and not infrastructure anybody operates. No
model is trained here and no benchmark is run.

NO AFFILIATION

This work is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by, or produced on
behalf of any company, university, research institute or funding programme.
Every paper, author, model and organisation named here is named because their
public work is the subject being catalogued. Naming them is citation, not
association, and none of them has reviewed or approved anything here.

WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT

Two different kinds of claim appear in this exercise and they must not be
confused.

MEASURED BY US: the arithmetic. The KV-cache formula, the T^2 growth in
attention scores, the ratio between MHA, GQA and MQA, and the effect of
sequence compression are computed in `src/attention/cache.py` from the
configuration in `config.py`. They are exact for that configuration -- 48
layers, 8 KV heads, head dimension 128, bf16 -- and meaningless for any other.
They are arithmetic, not observations of a running system: nothing here was
profiled, timed or benchmarked.

READ FROM SOURCES, NOT REPRODUCED: everything else. Each mechanism's date is
read from its primary source. Each mechanism's reported benefits -- a speedup, a
cache reduction, a quality result -- belongs to the paper that reported it and is
attributed there. We did not re-run any of them, and this exercise offers no
independent evidence that any published number is correct.

WRITTEN BY US, AND ARGUABLE: the trade-off fields. "What it buys", "what it
gives up" and "when would I actually choose it" are our editorial judgement,
formed from the sources and from the session. They are the part of this work a
reader should push back on, and no test can check them.

ON DATES

Every date is the first public appearance we could verify, read from the primary
source on the date recorded in `verified_on`, with the source's own wording
stored verbatim beside it. For arXiv papers that is the v1 submission-history
line rather than a later revision or a conference date.

A first-appearance date is not a claim of invention. Several entries have
precursors, and where the attribution is genuinely contested the entry records
the competing candidates rather than resolving them. Learned absolute position
embeddings in particular reach back at least to 2016, and a 2015 lead that we
did not open is noted as a lead rather than asserted as a source.

One entry -- NTK-aware RoPE scaling -- has no paper at all. It originates in a
public forum post, and its date is that platform's own timestamp field, read
from a web archive capture because the live site refused our requests. The entry
says so.

NO SOURCE TEXT IS REDISTRIBUTED

No paper is copied, mirrored, quoted at length or redistributed here. The
catalogue stores bibliographic metadata -- title, identifier, URL, the date
line -- and our own descriptions written from reading them. Short quotations
from the course material appear where the point is what the course actually
said, including where it was wrong.

WHERE THE COURSE MATERIAL IS WRONG, WE SAY SO

The assignment explicitly invites this. Two errors in the session are recorded
in README.md and CLAUDE.md with the corrected sources: the transformer's date,
and a confusion between two papers whose names differ by one capital letter. A
third discrepancy -- a cache figure that does not reproduce from the session's
own formula -- is recorded with both values rather than resolved in favour of
either.
